From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:11:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: optionally keep up to four render
 commands resident

macOS 26B123 keeps about 20 render commands resident in the firmware's
render rings (35 at most) and is notified of their completion about once
per 17.5 doorbells, J604-MEASURED from a hypervisor trace of the macOS
guest. That guest ran its own AGX firmware, not the 25G83 firmware this
driver runs. This driver keeps one render command in the firmware, or two
with g16_flights=2 / g16_depth=1.

Add g16_resident (default 0: off, unchanged behaviour). With 2..4, and
g16_overlap=1 and g16_uma_split=1 in effect, that many render commands may
be in flight at once, each in its own render pass unit (pass slot, UMA/heap
allocator, PB slot), retired in order as their receipts complete.

Today's bounds, and what changes with g16_resident:
- Firmware::ahead_ok() allowed flights().max(1 + depth()) with depth()
  capped at 1 and FLIGHTS = 2; it now allows the resident depth.
- FLIGHTS (and the flight vector reserved at startup) becomes RENDER_UNITS
  = 4; compute stays bounded by COMPUTE_SLOTS = 2 (g16_horizon).
- Order::ready_in_flight() allowed exactly one published, unretired
  same-engine predecessor; it now takes the number allowed (1 keeps the
  old rule; g16_resident allows depth - 1). A job's later commands still
  wait for its earlier ones (index 0 only).
- Each queue's scheduler had 2 credits; a queue created after
  g16_resident >= 3 was latched gets the latched number of units.
- Two render pass units per VM, with a two-owner lifetime ledger. The
  ledger now holds up to four owners and retires the oldest first among
  all of them. Every VM allocates the latched number of units: 2, or
  g16_resident (3..4) when it is set at module load. A resident unit binds
  its own split UMA slot (render 33 + k; the 25G83 layout has 32 per class)
  and PB slot k.
- Units 0 and 1 keep their placement. Resident units 2 and 3 take one
  512 MiB window each in 0x7080000000..0x70c0000000, the only free part
  of the client aperture. A unit backs its 256 MiB PB heap and 32 MiB
  pool in full (about 315 MiB per unit per VM), which is what bounds the
  depth at four. macOS-like depths (20 resident) need resident commands
  to share PB/UMA state, which has not been probed on 25G83.

The completion path already handles coalesced notifications: retire()
keeps retiring the oldest flight while its receipt is complete, so one
ring-service event retires every finished command in order. The receipts
of a flight with later flights behind it are proven against the newest
publication (stamp_reached, queue_receipt_behind), which holds for any
number of flights within the 64 unconsumed records AHEAD_RECORDS allows
per 256-entry ring.

A "resident:" stats line reports the depth, the latched units, the most
flights seen at once and the publications made behind two or more.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
